// The language pages
// ==================
// What `hive agents` writes into `.hivedocs/` about the language itself: one
// short page per feature, and an index in front of them. `library.hive` holds
// the `hive.*` half and `ui.hive` holds `hive.ui`.
//
// They are **short on purpose**. A page is read into an agent's context whole,
// so a page that says everything about everything costs the reader the whole of
// it to learn one thing. One page per feature, each complete about its own
// feature and silent about the rest, is what lets somebody read only what they
// are touching — and the index is what tells them which that is.
//
// They are **exact on purpose too**, which is the other half of the same job:
// what an agent cannot read, it guesses, and a guess about a language this
// strict compiles nowhere. Every rule here is one the compiler actually
// enforces, and the source of each is the chapter of `spec/` named at the foot
// of the page. **When the spec changes, these change with it.**
//
// A page is written the way `page.hive` says: a backtick string, with a marker
// where a backtick belongs.
import ./page

/// Every page about the language. `library.pages()` and `ui.pages()` are the
/// other halves, and `agents.hive` writes the three of them out together.
func pages(): page.Page[dyn] {
	return [
		page.Page("README.md", index()),
		page.Page("syntax.md", syntax()),
		page.Page("types.md", types()),
		page.Page("declarations.md", declarations()),
		page.Page("expressions.md", expressions()),
		page.Page("statements.md", statements()),
		page.Page("patterns.md", patterns()),
		page.Page("mutability.md", mutability()),
		page.Page("concurrency.md", concurrency()),
		page.Page("bounds.md", proofs()),
		page.Page("generics.md", generics()),
		page.Page("modules.md", modules()),
		page.Page("builtins.md", builtins()),
		page.Page("tables.md", tables()),
		page.Page("queries.md", queries()),
		page.Page("testing.md", testing()),
		page.Page("cli.md", cli()),
		page.Page("pitfalls.md", pitfalls())
	]
}

// --- The index ---------------------------------------------------------------

func index(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Hive, for whoever is writing it

		Hive is a memory-managed, table-based language that compiles to Go. Tables are
		a built-in idea rather than a library, values behave like values, and a great
		deal of what other languages leave to run time — every vector index, every
		branch of a match, the columns a SQL query comes back with — is settled while
		it compiles.

		Source files end in ~BT~.hive~BT~. The standard library is reached as ~BT~hive.*~BT~.

		## How to use these pages

		Each page is short and complete about one thing. **Read the page for what you
		are about to write** rather than guessing the syntax: Hive refuses a great deal
		that other languages accept, and every refusal below is one the compiler
		actually makes.

		| page | what is on it |
		| --- | --- |
		| [syntax](syntax.md) | comments, keywords, how a name must be spelled, both string forms |
		| [types](types.md) | scalars, vectors, ~BT~Result~BT~, declared types, maps, what may be assigned to what |
		| [declarations](declarations.md) | ~BT~type~BT~ ~BT~func~BT~ ~BT~proc~BT~ ~BT~query~BT~ ~BT~test~BT~, and returning on every path |
		| [expressions](expressions.md) | operators, precedence, calls, named arguments, function values |
		| [statements](statements.md) | bindings, assignment, ~BT~if~BT~, loops, ~BT~echo~BT~ / ~BT~assert~BT~ / ~BT~panic~BT~ |
		| [patterns](patterns.md) | ~BT~is~BT~, its four pattern kinds, narrowing, exhaustiveness |
		| [mutability](mutability.md) | ~BT~mut~BT~, mutex parameters, why a binding sometimes copies |
		| [concurrency](concurrency.md) | ~BT~async~BT~, the await-all, ~BT~with timeout~BT~ |
		| [bounds](bounds.md) | how every index and slice is proved in range |
		| [generics](generics.md) | type variables and monomorphization |
		| [modules](modules.md) | ~BT~import~BT~, the four path kinds, importing Go |
		| [builtins](builtins.md) | the functions that need no import |
		| [tables](tables.md) | ~BT~using~BT~: CSV, spreadsheets, and the ~BT~Table~BT~ type |
		| [queries](queries.md) | ~BT~query~BT~ declarations and the SQL in them |
		| [testing](testing.md) | ~BT~test~BT~, ~BT~assert~BT~, coverage |
		| [cli](cli.md) | every ~BT~hive~BT~ command |
		| [pitfalls](pitfalls.md) | **the mistakes that do not compile** — read this one first |
		| [stdlib/](stdlib/README.md) | every ~BT~hive.*~BT~ module, one page each |

		## Eight rules that catch everybody

		1. **There is no truthiness.** A condition is a ~BT~Bool~BT~ and nothing else.
		2. **~BT~!~BT~ inverts a ~BT~Bool~BT~ and nothing else**, and it binds tight: ~BT~!a && b~BT~ is
		   ~BT~(!a) && b~BT~, and a match may not be negated at all.
		3. **Every index must be proved in range** at compile time, or guarded with
		   ~BT~if v bounds i~BT~. See [bounds](bounds.md).
		4. **A slice's high bound is inclusive**: ~BT~v[1:3]~BT~ is three elements.
		5. **~BT~append~BT~ needs a ~BT~mut T[dyn]~BT~**, and ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ is declared, never inferred:
		   ~BT~mut v := ["a"]~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[1]~BT~ and cannot grow.
		6. **No number is widened for you.** ~BT~Int~BT~ does not become ~BT~Float~BT~; go through
		   ~BT~hive.conv.itf~BT~.
		7. **A name's spelling says what it is** — ~BT~camelCase~BT~ values, ~BT~PascalCase~BT~
		   types — and it is enforced. ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is *allowed* for an immutable
		   variable, never required: ~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default for every variable.
		8. **Every call blocks its caller.** Concurrency is decided where a call is
		   written, never where it is declared.

		## Where these came from

		Every page is condensed from ~BT~spec/~BT~ in the Hive repository, which is the
		normative reference. A page ends with the chapters it came from; read those
		when a page leaves a question open.
	`)
}

// --- The language ------------------------------------------------------------

func syntax(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Syntax

		A source file is UTF-8 text ending in ~BT~.hive~BT~. It has no header: what it
		declares is what it holds. Hive is **not** indentation-sensitive and no
		statement is terminated by a newline; a ~BT~;~BT~ on its own is skipped.

		~CODE~hive
		// A comment runs to the end of the line. There is no block comment.
		x := 1 // ...or takes the tail of one.
		~CODE~

		## Keywords

		Every keyword is lower case **and only lower case**:

		~CODE~
		import   proc     func     query    test     type
		if       else     return   is       using    with
		void     true     false    echo     assert   panic
		dyn      mut      async    await    for      in
		each     bounds   break    continue
		~CODE~

		A keyword written in another casing — ~BT~Proc~BT~, ~BT~IF~BT~, ~BT~Return~BT~ — is **neither a
		keyword nor a name**, and is a compile error naming both readings. That holds
		in *every* identifier position, so a union cannot have a variant called
		~BT~Return~BT~ or ~BT~Echo~BT~, and a type cannot have a field called ~BT~type~BT~.

		Six words are keywords only where they are written and are ordinary names
		everywhere else: ~BT~as~BT~ (after an import path), ~BT~run~BT~ and ~BT~raw~BT~ (after a ~BT~using~BT~
		connection), ~BT~csv~BT~ / ~BT~xlsx~BT~ / ~BT~ods~BT~ / ~BT~separating~BT~ / ~BT~by~BT~ (in a ~BT~using~BT~ clause),
		and ~BT~timeout~BT~ (in ~BT~with timeout~BT~). So a variable may be called ~BT~timeout~BT~.

		## Names have shapes, and the shape is enforced

		| shape | what it names |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~camelCase~BT~ | variables, parameters, fields, every ~BT~proc~BT~/~BT~func~BT~/~BT~query~BT~, an import's name |
		| ~BT~PascalCase~BT~ | types, their variants, and atoms |
		| ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ | **also** allowed for a variable nothing reassigns — a constant |

		Precisely: ~BT~camelCase~BT~ is a lower-case letter then ASCII letters and digits;
		~BT~PascalCase~BT~ is an upper-case letter then the same; ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is an
		upper-case letter then upper-case letters, digits and underscores.

		### ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is allowed, never required

		**~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default spelling of every variable, whether or not anything
		reassigns it.** ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is a second shape an *immutable* variable may take,
		for a value the program treats as a constant — and taking it is your choice:

		~CODE~hive
		retries := 3            // fine
		MAX_RETRIES := 3        // also fine, and says the same thing louder
		~CODE~

		Nothing is inferred from the choice, because **what makes a binding immutable is
		the absence of ~BT~mut~BT~, never its spelling**. Most immutable variables are written
		~BT~camelCase~BT~, being ordinary values that happen not to be reassigned rather than
		constants anybody would call one.

		The option belongs to **variables alone**: a parameter, a field, a callable, an
		import's name and a name bound by a pattern or a ~BT~for each~BT~ are ~BT~camelCase~BT~ and
		nothing else, however fixed the value behind them is.

		A **~BT~mut~BT~ variable may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~** — that shape claims constant about
		something about to be reassigned — and a counting loop's counter is held to the
		same rule. That is the one direction the rule runs in: ~BT~mut~BT~ rules the shape
		*out*, and nothing ever rules it in.

		Any name may open with a single ~BT~_~BT~ (~BT~_scratch~BT~, ~BT~_MAX~BT~); what follows is spelled
		as it would be without it, and the compiler asks nothing of the prefix. On its
		own, ~BT~_~BT~ throws a value away.

		## Literals

		~BT~42~BT~ is an ~BT~Int~BT~ (64-bit, signed; ~BT~-1~BT~ is unary minus applied to ~BT~1~BT~).
		~BT~1.0~BT~ is a ~BT~Float~BT~ — a leading or trailing dot is **not** a float.
		~BT~true~BT~ / ~BT~false~BT~ are ~BT~Bool~BT~. ~BT~#Ready~BT~ is an ~BT~Atom~BT~ and must be PascalCase.
		~BT~[a, b, c]~BT~ is a vector.

		## The two string forms

		**~BT~"..."~BT~** takes escapes and ~BT~{expression}~BT~ interpolation:

		| escape | means |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~\n~BT~ ~BT~\t~BT~ ~BT~\r~BT~ | newline, tab, carriage return |
		| ~BT~\"~BT~ ~BT~\\~BT~ | a quote, a backslash |
		| ~BT~\{~BT~ | a literal ~BT~{~BT~, not an interpolation |
		| ~BT~\X~BT~ | any other ~BT~X~BT~ is itself |

		A newline inside one is part of it. A backtick has no meaning in one, so
		~BT~"~BT~ + a backtick + ~BT~"~BT~ is how a backtick is written at all.

		## The operators

		| | |
		| --- | --- |
		| three-character | ~BT~...~BT~ (the vector-pattern rest marker) |
		| two-character | ~BT~:=~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ ~BT~**~BT~ ~BT~&&~BT~ ~BT~\|\|~BT~ ~BT~++~BT~ ~BT~--~BT~ ~BT~+=~BT~ ~BT~-=~BT~ ~BT~*=~BT~ ~BT~/=~BT~ |
		| one-character | ~BT~{~BT~ ~BT~}~BT~ ~BT~(~BT~ ~BT~)~BT~ ~BT~[~BT~ ~BT~]~BT~ ~BT~:~BT~ ~BT~;~BT~ ~BT~,~BT~ ~BT~.~BT~ ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~=~BT~ ~BT~+~BT~ ~BT~-~BT~ ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ ~BT~!~BT~ |

		The longest spelling that fits wins, so ~BT~...~BT~ is matched before ~BT~.~BT~ and ~BT~!=~BT~
		before ~BT~!~BT~ — which is what makes a ~BT~!~BT~ reaching the last row one standing on
		its own: the negation. See [expressions](expressions.md).

		**A backtick string** is raw: no escapes, no interpolation, may span lines, and
		**cannot contain a backtick**. Its indentation is removed at compile time —
		leading and trailing blank lines dropped, then the longest common leading
		whitespace of every non-blank line — so it says what it looks like:

		~CODE~hive
		func goSource(): Str {
			return ~BT~
				package hive

				func Assert(ok bool) { }
			~BT~   // opens at column 1: "package hive\n\nfunc Assert(ok bool) { }"
		}
		~CODE~

		A tab and a space each count as one character of indentation.

		## Two places the lexer stops reading Hive

		* After **~BT~query~BT~**, the ~BT~{ ... }~BT~ is raw SQL, captured to its matching brace and
		  dedented, with its ~BT~{param}~BT~ interpolations left for the parser.
		  See [queries](queries.md).
		* After **~BT~import~BT~**, what follows is a path rather than tokens: everything to
		  the next whitespace. A quoted path (~BT~import "./lib/my file"~BT~) needs no
		  escaping inside, and ~BT~as~BT~ after it still reads as ~BT~as~BT~.

		Inside a ~BT~query~BT~ body, SQL's own ~BT~--~BT~ and ~BT~/* */~BT~ comments are recognised, and
		SQL keywords keep SQL's upper-case convention.

		*From ~BT~spec/01-lexical.md~BT~ and ~BT~spec/02-grammar.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func types(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Types

		Every expression has a type known at compile time. There is no dynamic type,
		no ~BT~Any~BT~ and no reflection: where a type is needed and cannot be worked out,
		the program is rejected rather than deferred to run time.

		## The scalars

		| type | holds |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Str~BT~ | UTF-8 text |
		| ~BT~Int~BT~ | a 64-bit signed integer — exactly Go's ~BT~int~BT~; overflow **wraps** silently |
		| ~BT~Float~BT~ | a 64-bit float |
		| ~BT~Bool~BT~ | ~BT~true~BT~ or ~BT~false~BT~ |
		| ~BT~Atom~BT~ | an interned symbol, written ~BT~#Name~BT~ |
		| ~BT~void~BT~ | nothing — a **return type only**, never a variable, field or element |

		### ~BT~Str~BT~

		A ~BT~Str~BT~ is a sequence of **characters**, and ~BT~len~BT~ counts those. It holds bytes,
		so a binary file survives a read/write round trip, but it is addressed as text.

		* ~BT~s[0]~BT~ is its first **character** and yields a one-character ~BT~Str~BT~ — there is
		  no character type.
		* ~BT~s[1:3]~BT~ is characters one through three, **both bounds inclusive**.
		* Every index into one is proved at compile time, and a ~BT~Str~BT~ **never** has a
		  length the compiler knows — so it is always guarded or from ~BT~indexOf~BT~.
		* ~BT~s[0] = "x"~BT~ is a compile error. A ~BT~Str~BT~ is a value, not storage.

		~BT~split(s, "")~BT~ reaches every character at once and is how a string is walked
		without indexing it.

		### ~BT~Atom~BT~

		Written ~BT~#Name~BT~, interned to a small integer, declared nowhere. ~BT~echo~BT~ prints its
		name; coercing one to ~BT~Str~BT~ yields its decimal value. ~BT~#Nil~BT~ is the only atom the
		language provides and is always first on the table.

		**An atom is not a condition.** ~BT~if flag~BT~ on an ~BT~Atom~BT~ is a compile error; compare
		it with the one you mean, ~BT~if flag == #Ready~BT~. An atom cannot be computed.

		## ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~

		The one built-in generic union, and how every fallible operation reports
		itself: ~BT~Result.Ok(value)~BT~ and ~BT~Result.Error(payload)~BT~. Matching both variants
		is exhaustive. See [patterns](patterns.md).

		## Vectors

		Memory-contiguous and homogeneous. Three spellings, and **which one a name
		holds is what its declaration says, never what its value happened to show**:

		| written | means | legal where |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ | **static**: exactly three | anywhere |
		| ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ | **dynamic**: promises nothing about the length | anywhere |
		| ~BT~Str[]~BT~ | **any length** of this element type | **parameters only** |

		~BT~Str[]~BT~ is a parameter spelling and that is the whole of where it is legal: a
		return and anything naming storage must say which of the two real kinds it is.

		**Dynamic is declared, never inferred.** A ~BT~:=~BT~ binding reads its length off its
		value, and a length read off a value is a static one:

		~CODE~hive
		mut v := ["a", "b", "c"]
		append(v, "d")            // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is not a dynamic vector

		mut Str[dyn] w = ["a", "b", "c"]
		append(w, "d")            // fine
		~CODE~

		Operations: ~BT~+~BT~ concatenates into a **new** vector and adds the two lengths;
		~BT~==~BT~ compares structurally, element by element; ~BT~v[i]~BT~ indexes and ~BT~v[lo:hi]~BT~
		slices with ~BT~hi~BT~ **inclusive**. Comparing a vector to a non-vector is a compile
		error, not a silent ~BT~false~BT~. ~BT~Str[3][2]~BT~ is two vectors of three.

		~BT~Table~BT~ is an alias for ~BT~Str[dyn][dyn]~BT~ — a vector of rows of cells. See
		[tables](tables.md).

		## Declared types

		No variants is a struct; variants make a tagged union.

		~CODE~hive
		type User {                 // a struct
			id:   Int
			name: Str
		}

		type Shape {                // a tagged union
			Circle { radius: Int }
			Rectangle { width: Int, height: Int }
			Point                   // a variant may carry nothing
		}

		type Event {
			at: Int                 // outside every variant: added to all of them
			Opened { by: Str }
			Closed
		}
		~CODE~

		A field may carry ~BT~--~BT~ annotations saying what it is called in JSON and what
		to read where JSON has nothing — see
		[declarations](declarations.md#field-annotations).

		Built by calling the type or the variant — ~BT~User(1, "ada")~BT~, ~BT~Shape.Circle(5)~BT~,
		~BT~Shape.Point()~BT~ — and narrowed with ~BT~is~BT~. **There is no other way to reach a
		variant's fields**: a value typed as the union has only what every variant
		shares.

		A type may recurse **through a variant** (an expression tree does); a struct may
		not contain itself directly, since it would have no finite size.

		## Function types

		Written like a declaration with the name dropped: ~BT~func(Int): Int~BT~,
		~BT~proc(hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse~BT~. A ~BT~func~BT~ value may be used
		where a ~BT~proc~BT~ is expected; a ~BT~proc~BT~ value may not fill a ~BT~func~BT~ slot. ~BT~mut~BT~ may
		not appear in a function type.

		## Maps

		~BT~hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~ is the one collection that is not a vector, so it is a
		module rather than a literal ([stdlib/map](stdlib/map.md)). Three type-level
		rules:

		* A **key is compared and hashed whole**, so it is a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~,
		  ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~, or a declared type whose every field is one of those.
		  **Storage cannot be a key**: ~BT~Map<Str[dyn], Int>~BT~ is a compile error.
		* ~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ says *empty* and nothing about what it holds, so it must
		  land somewhere that says. ~BT~m := hive.map.new()~BT~ is a compile error.
		* A map is **reached by key, never by position**. ~BT~m[0]~BT~ is a compile error and
		  so is ~BT~for each~BT~ over one.

		## Assignability

		A value of type ~BT~S~BT~ fits where ~BT~T~BT~ is expected exactly when:

		1. they are the same type; or
		2. ~BT~T~BT~ is ~BT~T'[]~BT~, ~BT~S~BT~ is ~BT~T'[n]~BT~ or ~BT~T'[dyn]~BT~, and the element types match; or
		3. ~BT~T~BT~ is a ~BT~proc~BT~ type and ~BT~S~BT~ is the ~BT~func~BT~ type with the same shape; or
		4. ~BT~S~BT~ is ~BT~mut T~BT~ and ~BT~T~BT~ names no mutex — the callee gets an immutable copy.

		There is **no implicit numeric widening**: ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ is a
		compile error rather than a surprise, and ~BT~hive.conv.itf~BT~ is how an ~BT~Int~BT~
		becomes a ~BT~Float~BT~. There is **no subtyping and no interface**, and a variant is
		not a type of its own — ~BT~Shape.Circle~BT~ cannot be a parameter's type.

		A **declared static length is a promise**, so a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ slot only ever takes a
		vector of exactly three, wherever it came from. See [bounds](bounds.md).

		*From ~BT~spec/03-types.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func declarations(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Declarations

		A file holds imports and then declarations, in any order. There are five kinds
		and **no ~BT~pub~BT~/~BT~priv~BT~ distinction**: everything a module declares is visible to
		whoever imports it. Declarations at module level are mutually visible, so two
		callables may call each other.

		## ~BT~type~BT~

		See [types](types.md#declared-types) for the shapes. Every declared type gets,
		derived from its declaration and emitted only where used: a deep copy, a total
		order, a JSON codec and a structural digest. None of it is written by hand and
		none of it uses reflection.

		### Field annotations

		A field may be annotated, and both annotations are about the **JSON codec**
		derived above — what the field is called on the wire, and what to read where
		the wire has nothing:

		~CODE~hive
		type User {
			name: Str -- JSON as username;
			age:  Int
			firstName: Str -- Default as "";
		}
		~CODE~

		| written | means |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~JSON as <name>;~BT~ | the key this field is read from and written as |
		| ~BT~Default as <literal>;~BT~ | what to use where JSON has ~BT~null~BT~ or nothing at all |

		A ~BT~--~BT~ opens them and each clause ends at its ~BT~;~BT~, so **they may be written
		in any order** and a field may carry one, both or neither:

		~CODE~hive
		theme: Str -- JSON as colour_scheme; Default as "light";
		theme: Str -- Default as "light"; JSON as colour_scheme;   // the same field
		~CODE~

		They are **compile-time**, and nothing about the Hive side of the field
		changes: it is still ~BT~user.name~BT~ whatever JSON calls it, still ordered and
		copied and compared as it was.

		**~BT~JSON as~BT~ takes somebody else's name**, so it is held to none of Hive's own
		shapes — ~BT~user_name~BT~ is a key, and so is ~BT~"user-name"~BT~ written as a string
		where it is not a word at all. **Two fields of one object may not answer to one
		key**: that is a compile error, since the decoder would read the same member
		twice and the encoder would write it twice.

		**~BT~Default as~BT~ takes a literal** — an ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~ or
		~BT~Atom~BT~, with a leading ~BT~-~BT~ for a negative number — and it has to be a value
		the field can hold. What it stands in for is a key that is **absent** and a key
		that is present and **null**, which are one nothing; without it, either is an
		error. It stands in for nothing else, so a value of the **wrong type** is still
		an error: a default is for what was never said, not for what was said wrongly.

		~BT~--~BT~ is the same two characters the decrement step is written with, and there
		is nothing to tell apart — a step is a statement, and a type body holds no
		statements. It needs no space behind it, and ~BT~JSON~BT~, ~BT~Default~BT~ and ~BT~as~BT~ are
		not reserved words.

		## ~BT~func~BT~ and ~BT~proc~BT~

		~CODE~hive
		func greet(name: Str, style: Greeting): Str {
			if style is Greeting.Formal(title) {
				return "Good evening, {title} {name}."
			}
			return "Hey {name}!"
		}

		proc grow(vec: mut Str[dyn], tag: Str): void {
			append(vec, tag)
		}
		~CODE~

		A ~BT~func~BT~ **may perform I/O** — ~BT~echo~BT~, ~BT~using~BT~, ~BT~hive.net~BT~ are all allowed. It
		differs from a ~BT~proc~BT~ in exactly two ways:

		1. it cannot declare a mutex parameter (~BT~v: mut T~BT~) — a ~BT~mut~BT~ value passed to a
		   ~BT~func~BT~ is seen as an ordinary immutable copy instead; and
		2. **it cannot call a ~BT~proc~BT~**.

		So what a ~BT~func~BT~ promises is not mathematical purity but that it cannot write to
		storage its caller can see. A ~BT~proc~BT~ may do everything a ~BT~func~BT~ may, plus both of
		those. Programs start at ~BT~proc main(): void~BT~, in the file handed to
		~BT~hive build~BT~ / ~BT~hive run~BT~.

		Parameters are ~BT~name: Type~BT~, or ~BT~name: mut Type~BT~ in a ~BT~proc~BT~. Every callable
		declares a return type, ~BT~void~BT~ included.

		## Returning on every path

		Every non-~BT~void~BT~ ~BT~proc~BT~ and ~BT~func~BT~ must return on every path. A path terminates
		by ending in:

		* ~BT~return~BT~;
		* ~BT~assert~BT~ or ~BT~panic~BT~ — both handy for a tail you know is unreachable;
		* an ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else~BT~ whose every branch terminates; or
		* an **else-less** ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain that covers its subject's whole type — a
		  ~BT~Result~BT~'s ~BT~Ok~BT~ and ~BT~Error~BT~, or every variant of a declared union.

		~CODE~hive
		func describe(shape: Shape): Str {
			if shape is Shape.Circle(r)            { return "circle" }
			else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, h) { return "rectangle" }
			else if shape is Shape.Point           { return "a point" }
		}                                          // no ~BT~else~BT~ — adding one is an error
		~CODE~

		## ~BT~query~BT~

		A ~BT~func~BT~ whose body is inline SQL and whose return type describes its rows. It
		has a page of its own: [queries](queries.md).

		## ~BT~test~BT~

		~CODE~hive
		test "an empty cart costs nothing" {
			Item[0] empty = []
			assert total(empty) == 0
		}
		~CODE~

		Named in prose, takes no parameters, returns nothing. ~BT~return value~BT~ inside one
		is a compile error; a bare ~BT~return~BT~ to leave early is fine. See
		[testing](testing.md).

		## ~BT~import~BT~

		Written outside any callable. See [modules](modules.md).

		## Nothing about a declaration says how it runs

		**Every call blocks its caller.** There is no ~BT~async func~BT~, no Future and no
		Promise type to name; what a call means is decided where it is written
		([concurrency](concurrency.md)). The two callable kinds are about *what a body
		may do*, never about *when it happens*.

		*From ~BT~spec/04-declarations.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func expressions(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Expressions

		## Precedence, loosest to tightest

		| level | operators | associativity |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| 1 | ~BT~\|\|~BT~ | left |
		| 2 | ~BT~&&~BT~ | left |
		| 3 | ~BT~is~BT~, ~BT~bounds~BT~ | non-associative |
		| 4 | ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ | non-associative |
		| 5 | ~BT~+~BT~ ~BT~-~BT~ | left |
		| 6 | ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ | left |
		| 7 | unary ~BT~-~BT~, ~BT~!~BT~ | prefix |
		| 8 | ~BT~**~BT~ | right |
		| 9 | a ~BT~with~BT~ clause | postfix |
		| 10 | call, index, slice, member | postfix, left |

		Unary ~BT~-~BT~ binds **tighter** than ~BT~* / %~BT~ and **looser** than ~BT~**~BT~, so ~BT~-2 ** 2~BT~
		is ~BT~-(2 ** 2)~BT~ and ~BT~2 ** -3~BT~ reads the sign as part of the exponent. ~BT~!~BT~ binds
		at that same level.

		## What the operators do

		| operator | on | yields |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| ~BT~+~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ / ~BT~Str~BT~ / vectors | the sum / concatenation / a **new** vector |
		| ~BT~-~BT~ ~BT~*~BT~ ~BT~/~BT~ ~BT~%~BT~ ~BT~**~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ | arithmetic |
		| unary ~BT~!~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | the opposite |
		| ~BT~==~BT~ ~BT~!=~BT~ | any two values of **one** type | structural equality |
		| ~BT~<~BT~ ~BT~>~BT~ ~BT~<=~BT~ ~BT~>=~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~ | ordering |
		| ~BT~&&~BT~ ~BT~\|\|~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ | short-circuiting |
		| ~BT~is~BT~ | see [patterns](patterns.md) | a ~BT~Bool~BT~, binding as it matches |
		| ~BT~bounds~BT~ | a vector and an ~BT~Int~BT~ | sugar for ~BT~i >= 0 && i < hive.len(v)~BT~ |

		Comparing values of two different types is a compile error, not a silent
		~BT~false~BT~, and there is **no implicit numeric conversion** anywhere.

		## Unary ~BT~!~BT~

		It takes a **~BT~Bool~BT~** and answers with the opposite one. Anything else is a
		compile error, for the reason a condition may not be one: there is no
		truthiness, so there is nothing about an ~BT~Int~BT~, a ~BT~Str~BT~ or an ~BT~Atom~BT~ to be the
		opposite of.

		It binds where unary ~BT~-~BT~ binds, which is **tighter than every comparison and
		every conjunction**:

		~CODE~hive
		if !found { }                  // the whole of what it is for
		echo !isEven(3)                // the call is the operand, not ~BT~isEven~BT~
		if !found && ready { }         // (!found) && ready
		if !(a == b) { }               // parentheses, because ~BT~==~BT~ is looser
		if !(v bounds i) { }           // the same, and this proves nothing about ~BT~i~BT~
		~CODE~

		It is not the only way to say it and does not make the others wrong:
		~BT~found == false~BT~ is an ordinary comparison and
		~BT~indexOf(v, x) is Result.Error(_)~BT~ an ordinary match.

		### A match cannot be negated

		**~BT~!~BT~ may not be applied to an expression holding an ~BT~is~BT~**, and that is the one
		~BT~Bool~BT~ it refuses:

		~CODE~hive
		if !(x is Shape.Circle(r)) { }        // compile error
		if !(ready && (x is Shape.Point)) { } // the same, through the ~BT~&&~BT~
		~CODE~

		The reason is what ~BT~is~BT~ does beyond answering: it **narrows** the value it
		matched and **binds** parts of it, and neither survives being negated — the
		branch under a negated match is the branch where the pattern did *not* hold, so
		~BT~r~BT~ above could never be set.

		**The other case goes in the ~BT~else~BT~**, which is where it always went. The
		refusal reaches through ~BT~&&~BT~, ~BT~||~BT~ and a second ~BT~!~BT~ and no further, and a ~BT~!~BT~
		*beside* a match is untouched: ~BT~!a && (x is P)~BT~ negates ~BT~a~BT~ and nothing else.

		## Interpolation

		~BT~"{expr}"~BT~ splices a value into a string, rendering a non-~BT~Str~BT~ piece the way
		~BT~echo~BT~ does. ~BT~\{~BT~ is a literal brace. **This is the only implicit conversion in
		the language, and it goes one way**: a value becomes text, never the reverse.

		~CODE~hive
		echo "loaded {len(rows)} of {total}"
		~CODE~

		## Calls and named arguments

		Funcs, procs, queries and type constructors — builtin ones included — accept
		arguments by name:

		~CODE~hive
		f(b: 1, "s")
		~CODE~

		Named arguments may appear anywhere; only the unnamed ones need to be in order.
		Names must exist, must not repeat, and **once named arguments are used the call
		must cover the full parameter list**.

		## Function values

		A callable is a value you can pass, store and call later. Two spellings:

		~CODE~hive
		f := takes                              // a bare reference
		handler(_, db)                          // a partial application
		hive.net.httpServe(8080, handler(_, db))
		~CODE~

		Each ~BT~_~BT~ becomes a parameter of the resulting function, **in hole order**, and
		everything supplied is captured by value where it was written. A **constructor**
		may be partially applied and means the same: ~BT~Msg.Changed(_)~BT~ is a
		~BT~func(Str): Msg~BT~.

		Three things cannot become values: a **generic** callable, a callable with a
		**mutex parameter**, and a callable with a **statically-sized parameter** beyond
		one immutable local binding it is called through.

		## Member access, indexing, slicing

		~BT~x.field~BT~ reads a field; ~BT~x.member~BT~ reads through an import alias, the two told
		apart by what the left side names. ~BT~v[i]~BT~ indexes; ~BT~v[lo:hi]~BT~ slices with **both
		bounds inclusive** — ~BT~t[1:3]~BT~ is three elements — and either bound may be
		omitted (~BT~v[:2]~BT~, ~BT~v[1:]~BT~, ~BT~v[:]~BT~). Every one is proved in range at compile
		time ([bounds](bounds.md)).

		## Arithmetic at the edges

		| expression | result |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~a / 0~BT~, ~BT~a % 0~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~ — division by zero is a value, not a crash |
		| ~BT~Int~BT~ overflow | wraps, two's-complement, silently |
		| ~BT~2 ** 100~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~ — that wrap, reached by repeated multiplication |
		| ~BT~n ** k~BT~, ~BT~k < 0~BT~ on ~BT~Int~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~, including ~BT~1 ** -1~BT~ |
		| ~BT~n ** 0~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~ |
		| ~BT~-7 % 3~BT~ | ~BT~-1~BT~ — the remainder takes the sign of the dividend |
		| ~BT~10.0 ** 400.0~BT~ | ~BT~+Inf~BT~ — ~BT~Float~BT~ arithmetic does produce non-finite values |

		Converting a non-finite or too-large ~BT~Float~BT~ to an ~BT~Int~BT~ is the **one**
		unspecified case in the language; check the range first if it matters.

		## ~BT~with~BT~ clauses

		Two unrelated clauses share the keyword, told apart by the word after it.

		* **~BT~with timeout <ms>~BT~** bounds a wait and turns its result into
		  ~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~. See [concurrency](concurrency.md).
		* **~BT~with <Type>~BT~** names a decode target:
		  ~BT~hive.json.parse(text) with User~BT~,
		  ~BT~hive.crypto.jwtVerify(token, secret) with Claims~BT~.

		## Evaluation order

		Operands are evaluated left to right, and ~BT~&&~BT~ / ~BT~||~BT~ short-circuit — which is
		what makes ~BT~if x is T.A(v) && v == "ok"~BT~ legal. Every expression is evaluated
		**exactly once** per time control reaches it, an ~BT~assert~BT~'s operand included.

		*From ~BT~spec/05-expressions.md~BT~ and ~BT~spec/02-grammar.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func statements(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Statements

		## Bindings

		~CODE~hive
		x := expr                    // inferred
		Str[dyn] v = expr            // annotated
		mut x := expr                // inferred, reassignable
		mut Str[dyn] v = expr        // annotated, reassignable
		_ := expr                    // throws the value away
		~CODE~

		~BT~:=~BT~ infers the type from the value, **including a static vector length**.
		~BT~=~BT~ with a type in front states it, and is the **only** way to say ~BT~[dyn]~BT~, to
		say ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ as a promise rather than an observation, or to give a
		~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ somewhere to land.

		A binding is **immutable** unless it says ~BT~mut~BT~. An immutable name may not be
		reassigned, may not be written through (~BT~v[i] = …~BT~, ~BT~v.f = …~BT~), and may not be
		~BT~append~BT~ed to.

		**~BT~mut~BT~ is what decides that, never the spelling.** An immutable variable is
		written ~BT~camelCase~BT~ by default and **may** be written ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ instead,
		which is how a constant is spelled — ~BT~retries := 3~BT~ and ~BT~MAX_RETRIES := 3~BT~ hold
		their values in exactly the same way. See [syntax](syntax.md).

		## Assignment

		~CODE~hive
		x = expr
		v[i] = expr
		rec.field = expr
		b.items[0] = expr
		~CODE~

		The target must be a ~BT~mut~BT~ binding or a path into one. Assigning to a parameter
		or to a plain ~BT~:=~BT~ binding is a compile error. ~BT~+= -= *= /=~BT~ and the steps
		~BT~++~BT~ / ~BT~--~BT~ are shorthand for the matching ~BT~x = x <op> …~BT~.

		## ~BT~if~BT~

		~CODE~hive
		if cond { } else if cond { } else { }
		~CODE~

		The condition is a ~BT~Bool~BT~. **There is no truthiness in Hive** — an ~BT~Atom~BT~, an
		~BT~Int~BT~, a ~BT~Str~BT~ and a vector are each a compile error there. Names bound by an
		~BT~is~BT~ in the condition are in scope in that branch's body and in the rest of the
		same condition after ~BT~&&~BT~.

		## Loops

		~CODE~hive
		for i := 0; i < 10; i++ { }
		for ; cond; { }                      // any clause may be omitted: a while loop

		for each name in values { }
		for each name: T in values { }       // the annotation overrides inference
		~CODE~

		The counter is scoped to the loop and **implicitly mutable**, so it needs no
		~BT~mut~BT~ — and is held to the ~BT~mut~BT~ naming rule, so it may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~.
		Neither the init nor the post clause may be ~BT~async~BT~.

		~BT~for each~BT~ walks a vector, binding each element to an **immutable** name. It
		never indexes, so it needs no bounds proof. ~BT~for each~BT~ over a map is a compile
		error.

		~BT~break~BT~ leaves the innermost enclosing loop; ~BT~continue~BT~ skips to its next
		iteration. Either outside a loop is a compile error.

		## ~BT~echo~BT~, ~BT~assert~BT~, ~BT~panic~BT~

		~BT~echo value~BT~ writes one line to stdout: an atom prints its **name**, a vector
		its elements, a map its pairs in its own order, a declared value its fields. It
		is legal in a ~BT~func~BT~ and a ~BT~proc~BT~ alike.

		~BT~assert cond~BT~ says this must hold, and **where it is written decides what
		happens when it does not**: in ordinary code a failure has proved the *program*
		wrong, so it stops; inside a ~BT~test~BT~ it has proved the *test* wrong, so the
		failure is recorded and the suite carries on. Its operands are evaluated exactly
		once either way.

		~BT~panic value~BT~ stops the program immediately, rendering ~BT~value~BT~ exactly as ~BT~echo~BT~
		would. It takes any value rather than only a ~BT~Bool~BT~, and never returns — so a
		branch ending in one counts as a terminating path. The single exception to
		"stops the program" is inside a [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](stdlib/syslink.md) service,
		where a panic kills only that service.

		## ~BT~async~BT~ as a statement

		~CODE~hive
		async f(x)
		~CODE~

		Runs the call on its own virtual thread and carries on. See
		[concurrency](concurrency.md).

		## Expression statements

		A call may stand alone, and its result is discarded. Two builtins mean something
		particular there:

		* a discarded **~BT~sort~BT~** on a ~BT~mut~BT~ vector sorts it **in place**, and one that
		  cannot is a compile error rather than dead code;
		* a discarded **~BT~drop~BT~** is the ordinary way to remove elements.

		An expression that is not a call and has no effect is not a statement.

		## Scope

		A block introduces a scope. A name is visible from its declaration to the end of
		its block, and shadowing an outer name is allowed — a local binding shadows a
		module-level declaration and a builtin alike. **There is no forward reference
		inside a body**: a statement may only name what is already in scope.

		*From ~BT~spec/06-statements.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func patterns(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Patterns

		~BT~is~BT~ checks a value against a pattern. It is an ordinary expression yielding a
		~BT~Bool~BT~, so **every match is a plain ~BT~if~BT~ / ~BT~else if~BT~ — there is no ~BT~match~BT~ or
		~BT~case~BT~ statement**. When it matches it narrows the value and binds parts of it
		to fresh names, usable in the rest of the same condition after ~BT~&&~BT~ and in the
		branch body.

		Four kinds of thing can be matched.

		## Variant patterns

		~CODE~hive
		value is Type.Variant(a, b)
		value is Type.Variant          // a variant that carries nothing

		if shape is Shape.Circle(r) {
			echo r
		} else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, _) {
			echo w
		}
		~CODE~

		Fields are bound **by position**; ~BT~_~BT~ matches one without binding it. A partly
		written argument list is not allowed: **name every field or none**. An imported
		type is matched through the name its module is reached by
		(~BT~style is text.Align.Left~BT~).

		## ~BT~Result~BT~ patterns

		~CODE~hive
		if indexOf(names, "bob") is Result.Ok(i) { }     // the call runs once
		if parsed is Result.Error(problem) { }
		~CODE~

		## Vector patterns

		~CODE~hive
		v is ["a", x]                  // exactly two; first equals "a", second binds
		v is ["a", x, ...rest]         // at least two; ~BT~rest~BT~ binds the leftovers
		~CODE~

		Each position is a **literal** to match (~BT~"a"~BT~, ~BT~3~BT~, ~BT~#Atom~BT~, ~BT~true~BT~), a **name**
		to bind, or ~BT~_~BT~ to skip. A trailing ~BT~...rest~BT~ relaxes the length from an exact
		count to a lower bound; without one, the pattern matches only a vector of
		exactly that length.

		## String patterns

		A template: literal text that must match verbatim, plus holes that bind the text
		spanning each one.

		~CODE~hive
		path is "/health"                        // hole-less: an exact match
		path is "/users/{id}/posts/{postId}"
		path is "/files/{rest}"                  // a trailing hole runs to the end
		~CODE~

		* The template must cover the **whole** string.
		* Matching is **non-greedy**, so a hole between two ~BT~/~BT~ never swallows a ~BT~/~BT~.
		* Holes must be plain binding names, and two **open** holes may not sit side by
		  side — the split point would be ambiguous. Both are compile errors.

		### A hole may say what it takes

		~BT~{name}~BT~ takes any text; ~BT~{name is (regex)}~BT~ takes only text matching the
		regex, and binds it the same way:

		~CODE~hive
		if line is "{user is (\w+)}@{host is ([\w.]+)}" {
			echo "{user} at {host}"
		}
		if path is "/api/v{version is (\d+)}/{rest}" { }
		~CODE~

		**Nothing inside the parentheses is escaped by the string**, so ~BT~{year is (\d{4})}~BT~
		is four digits rather than an interpolation of ~BT~4~BT~. That is also why a hole is
		written in a ~BT~"..."~BT~ pattern and never a backtick one.

		**The regex is read at compile time**, so a malformed one is a compile error. The
		syntax is Go's RE2: **no backreference and no lookaround**, both refused by name.
		A group inside a hole groups and does not capture — the hole is already the
		binding — and a named group is a compile error for the same reason.

		## Exhaustiveness

		An **else-less** ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain that covers its subject's whole type is a
		terminating path, which is what lets a total function over a union be written
		without a dead ~BT~else~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		func describe(shape: Shape): Str {
			if shape is Shape.Circle(r)            { return "circle" }
			else if shape is Shape.Rectangle(w, h) { return "rectangle" }
			else if shape is Shape.Point           { return "a point" }
		}
		~CODE~

		The whole type means every variant of a declared union, or a ~BT~Result~BT~'s ~BT~Ok~BT~ and
		~BT~Error~BT~. Every branch must test the **same** subject. **Vector and string
		patterns never make a chain exhaustive** — no finite set of them covers every
		string or every length.

		## Narrowing and scope

		A binding introduced by ~BT~is~BT~ is **immutable**, is in scope for the rest of the
		condition after ~BT~&&~BT~ and for the branch body, and is **out of scope in an
		~BT~else~BT~**, where the pattern did not match. Narrowing is per branch: outside one,
		a union value still has only the fields every variant shares.

		**A match cannot be negated.** ~BT~!(x is Shape.Circle(r))~BT~ is a compile error,
		because narrowing and binding are exactly what a ~BT~!~BT~ would throw away — the
		branch under one is the branch where the pattern did *not* hold, so ~BT~r~BT~ could
		never be set. The other case goes in the ~BT~else~BT~. See
		[expressions](expressions.md).

		*From ~BT~spec/07-patterns.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func mutability(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Mutability and value semantics

		## ~BT~mut~BT~

		Variables are immutable by default. ~BT~mut~BT~ allows reassignment, writing through
		(~BT~v[0] = …~BT~, ~BT~rec.f = …~BT~) and ~BT~append~BT~.

		Conceptually a ~BT~mut T~BT~ is a **~BT~Mutex<T>~BT~**: identical to ~BT~T~BT~ at run time, but
		only mutexes may be altered at compile time. A parameter or return of type ~BT~T~BT~
		accepts a ~BT~Mutex<T>~BT~ — the callee just sees an immutable ~BT~T~BT~, copied on the way
		in — never the reverse.

		~BT~mut~BT~ may not appear anywhere else a type can: **not on a field, not on a
		return, not in a ~BT~proc(...)~BT~ type**. A ~BT~mut~BT~ variable may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~.

		## Mutex parameters

		A **~BT~proc~BT~ — and only a ~BT~proc~BT~** — may ask for the mutex itself:

		~CODE~hive
		proc grow(vec: mut Str[dyn], tag: Str): void {
			append(vec, tag)
		}
		~CODE~

		The argument must then be a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable or a path into one, and the callee
		gets **that storage**: it may reassign it, write its elements and ~BT~append~BT~ to it,
		all visible through the caller's name afterwards.

		**Whether it shares depends on the call site, not the declaration:**

		~CODE~hive
		mut Str[dyn] v = ["a"]
		grow(v, "b")          // waited for: v is now ["a", "b"]
		async grow(v, "c")    // fired off: the thread grows a copy; v is untouched
		~CODE~

		A call you wait for *there* hands over the caller's storage; an ~BT~async~BT~ or
		~BT~await~BT~ed one hands over a copy — a thread of its own gets storage of its own.

		A callable with a mutex parameter can be neither referenced (~BT~f~BT~) nor partially
		applied (~BT~f(1, _)~BT~): a function value has no call site to take a mutex from.
		And passing a variable to a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter costs it every length and index fact
		[already proved](bounds.md) about it, since the callee may rebind it shorter.

		## What a value is

		Vectors, ~BT~Table~BT~s, maps and declared types that contain them are **value
		types**. Two names for one value never observe each other's mutations — unless
		both are ~BT~mut~BT~, which is how shared mutable state is opted into. The invariant
		the whole of it rests on:

		> Storage that an immutable binding observes is never mutated in place
		> afterwards.

		## Copy-on-binding

		A binding whose right-hand side **names existing storage** (~BT~ys := xs~BT~,
		~BT~ys := xs[i]~BT~, ~BT~ys := rec.field~BT~) may need to copy. A **fresh** right-hand side
		— a literal, a ~BT~+~BT~ concatenation, a function result — is already independent and
		is never copied.

		| target ⟵ source | decision |
		| --- | --- |
		| immutable ⟵ immutable | **alias** — neither side can ever mutate it |
		| ~BT~mut~BT~ ⟵ ~BT~mut~BT~ | **alias** — shared mutable state is the intent |
		| ~BT~mut~BT~ ⟵ immutable | **alias** if the target is never written through, else **copy** |
		| immutable ⟵ ~BT~mut~BT~ | **alias** if the source is never mutated again, else **copy** |

		An alias is only chosen when it is provably indistinguishable from a copy, and
		the analysis is deliberately conservative. An in-place ~BT~sort~BT~ counts as a write:

		~CODE~hive
		mut Int[dyn] a = [3, 1, 2]
		b := a          // copies, because...
		sort(a)         // ...this rewrites ~BT~a~BT~, and ~BT~b~BT~ must not follow
		~CODE~

		### Two ~BT~mut~BT~ bindings share completely

		~CODE~hive
		mut Str[dyn] a = ["x", "y", "z"]
		mut Str[dyn] b = a
		append(b, "w")            // len(a) is now 4
		a[0] = "changed"          // b[0] is "changed" too
		b = ["replaced"]          // rebinding one rebinds both; len(a) is 1
		~CODE~

		The second name is not given a variable at all — it compiles to the first, so
		there is one slice header for both. The one case this does not cover is a source
		that does not name the same storage every time it is read: ~BT~mut b = a[i]~BT~ keeps
		a header of its own.

		## What a copy copies

		Deep and **type-directed** — chosen by the static type, with no runtime
		reflection. A flat vector copies its backing array; a nested vector or ~BT~Table~BT~
		copies every level; a map copies its key order and its lookup; a declared type
		copies its storage-owning fields through a generated clone, and a scalar-only
		type needs nothing.

		A ~BT~mut T~BT~ parameter is the one place storage crosses a call boundary on purpose,
		and it lowers to a pointer — a shared backing array would not survive ~BT~append~BT~.

		*From ~BT~spec/08-mutability-and-values.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func concurrency(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Concurrency

		**Every call blocks its caller.** Nothing about a declaration says otherwise, so
		there is no ~BT~async func~BT~, no Future and no Promise type to name. What a call
		means is decided **where it is written**.

		## The four call sites

		### ~BT~f(x)~BT~ — wait for it

		Yields ~BT~T~BT~. It costs neither a goroutine nor a channel: it is a function call.

		### ~BT~async f(x)~BT~ — as a statement

		Runs the call on its own virtual thread and carries on. Fire-and-forget is the
		whole of it: the result is discarded and nothing is left behind to read one
		from. It works on any call — a ~BT~func~BT~, a ~BT~proc~BT~, a ~BT~query~BT~, the standard
		library, a service — and refuses three by name: a **global builtin**, a
		**constructor**, and a **partial application**.

		### ~BT~x := async f(a)~BT~ — keep the result

		Starts the call the same way and keeps its result. **The wait happens wherever
		~BT~x~BT~ is read**, and only if the call has not finished by then.

		~CODE~hive
		rows  := async loadRows()              // Str[dyn] — starts now
		total := async countAll()              // Int      — starts now, alongside
		echo "loaded {len(rows)} of {total}"   // waits for both, here
		~CODE~

		~BT~x~BT~'s type is the call's return type with **nothing wrapped around it** — ~BT~Str~BT~,
		not a Future of one. There is no handle type in the language and nothing to
		unwrap.

		* Reading ~BT~x~BT~ again is free and always answers the same value.
		* A panic inside the call is raised at the **first read**; if nothing ever reads
		  ~BT~x~BT~ the work still runs and the panic is dropped with the result.
		* It refuses the same three calls ~BT~async~BT~ does, and a ~BT~void~BT~ call besides.
		* ~BT~mut~BT~ cannot be combined with it, and neither can ~BT~with timeout~BT~.

		### ~BT~await [f(a), f(b), f(c)]~BT~ — the await-all

		The **only** ~BT~await~BT~ there is. Every call starts on its own thread and the whole
		list is one barrier, resolving **in order** to a statically-sized, fully-typed
		vector of their results (~BT~Str[3]~BT~) — never a dynamic or ~BT~Any~BT~ vector. Three
		calls that each take a second take about a second.

		* A list of one is legal and still means "on its own thread, then wait".
		* Over ~BT~void~BT~ calls the barrier has no value and is a **statement**.
		* Every entry has to be a **call**, and they must all answer with the **same
		  type**. ~BT~await []~BT~ is a compile error.

		Several calls that answer differently are given **names** instead — the ~BT~async~BT~
		binding above.

		## ~BT~with timeout~BT~

		Any wait **written where it happens** may be bounded:

		~CODE~hive
		f(x) with timeout 500
		await [f(a), f(b)] with timeout 500

		if slowShout("worth waiting for") with timeout 100 is Result.Error(err) {
			echo "gave up after {err.waited}ms"
		}
		~CODE~

		Without it, the wait yields the value; with it,
		~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~, whose error carries ~BT~waited~BT~ and a
		~BT~message~BT~.

		* On an await-all it is **one deadline across the whole barrier**.
		* A timeout abandons the **waiting, not the work**: a virtual thread cannot be
		  stopped from outside, so the call runs on and only its result is dropped.
		* A ~BT~void~BT~ call has no value for the ~BT~Result~BT~ to carry, so bounding one is
		  refused.
		* On a [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](stdlib/syslink.md) request the clause folds into that
		  module's own error rather than wrapping a second ~BT~Result~BT~ around the first.

		## Threads and mutex arguments

		A call handed a mutex argument shares the caller's storage **only when the
		caller waits for it there**. An ~BT~async~BT~ or ~BT~await~BT~ed call gets a copy, made on
		the caller's side of the fence. See [mutability](mutability.md).

		## What is deliberately absent

		No channel type, no mutex primitive, no thread handle, no cancellation token and
		no scheduler to configure. A program that needs long-lived shared state reaches
		for a [service](stdlib/syslink.md), whose handler is a fold over its mailbox and
		therefore needs no lock at all; a program that needs a result from work in
		flight names it.

		*From ~BT~spec/09-concurrency.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func proofs(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Bounds

		A dedicated flow-sensitive pass proves **every index and slice in range at
		compile time**, so the generated code can never fail out of bounds. Anything the
		pass cannot prove safe is a **compile error**, not a runtime crash.

		## What counts as a proof

		An access is safe when one of these holds:

		* the vector's length is **statically known** and the index is a literal within
		  it — ~BT~v[2]~BT~ on a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ compiles, ~BT~v[3]~BT~ does not;
		* it sits under a **guard the pass can read**:
		  ~BT~if i >= 0 && i < len(v) { v[i] }~BT~, ~BT~if v bounds i { v[i] }~BT~ (the same
		  guard, spelled shorter), or the condition of a counting ~BT~for~BT~ loop;
		* the index came from **~BT~indexOf~BT~**;
		* the access is a **~BT~for each~BT~**, which never indexes.

		A **computed** index is not provable and must be bound to a variable and
		guarded:

		~CODE~hive
		echo v[len(v) - 1]                  // compile error: a computed index

		last := len(v) - 1
		if v bounds last { echo v[last] }   // fine
		~CODE~

		## A known length survives what cannot lose it

		| operation | length of the result |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~a + b~BT~ | the two added — ~BT~Str[2] + Str[8]~BT~ fills a ~BT~Str[10]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~map(v, f)~BT~ | ~BT~v~BT~'s, unchanged |
		| ~BT~sort(v)~BT~ / ~BT~sort(v, first)~BT~ | ~BT~v~BT~'s, unchanged |
		| ~BT~filter~BT~, ~BT~filterMap~BT~ | **unknown, always** |
		| ~BT~split~BT~, ~BT~hive.map.keys~BT~, most library calls | unknown |

		They compose, so ~BT~map(a, f) + sort(b)~BT~ is as long as ~BT~a + b~BT~. If **either**
		side of a concatenation is unknown the result is unknown — there is no "at least
		this many". ~BT~filter~BT~ claims nothing because what is knowable about it is a
		*maximum*, and a maximum can never put an index in range.

		## A declared length is a promise

		~BT~Str[3]~BT~ means three, so **every** value landing in such a slot must be a vector
		of exactly that many — an initialiser, a later assignment, an argument, a field
		of a constructed value, a returned value:

		~CODE~hive
		mut Str[3] v = ["a", "b", "c"]
		v = ["x", "y", "z"]              // fine — still three
		v = ["x"]                        // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is declared ~BT~Str[3]~BT~
		Str[3] parts = split(line, ",")  // compile error: that length isn't known
		~CODE~

		Because the promise is kept everywhere it is never lost: ~BT~v[2]~BT~ stays legal
		after the reassignment, and a ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ parameter is indexed inside the callee
		without a guard.

		**A promise restricts a callable as a value.** One with a statically-sized
		parameter may be bound to an **immutable name** and called through it — those
		calls are checked exactly as direct ones are — but may **not** be handed on any
		further: passed as an argument, returned, or stored in a vector or field.
		Declaring the parameter ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ or ~BT~Str[]~BT~ lifts every restriction, at the
		cost of guarding the index inside the callee.

		## An inferred length is weaker

		It survives a write *through* the name and dies the moment the name is rebound —
		including in a branch that may not even run:

		~CODE~hive
		mut v := ["a", "b", "c"]
		v[0] = "x"                  // still three
		if changed { v = ["x"] }
		echo v[2]                   // compile error: v's length is no longer known
		~CODE~

		The same applies to a field: replacing it costs whatever had been proven about
		the old value. A ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ **field** is enforced wherever a value reaches it, so
		it can be indexed unguarded.

		## ~BT~indexOf~BT~ returns an index you can use

		~CODE~hive
		found := indexOf(names, "bob")
		if found is Result.Ok(i) {
			echo "{i}: {names[i]}"   // no guard needed
		}
		~CODE~

		The proof is tied to the vector that was searched, and only while that vector
		means the same thing: an index found in ~BT~a~BT~ still needs a guard to index ~BT~b~BT~.

		## What costs a proof

		* **rebinding** the vector, including in a branch that may not run;
		* **~BT~drop~BT~**, the one builtin that makes a vector shorter;
		* passing the vector to a **~BT~mut~BT~ parameter~BT~**, since the callee may rebind it;
		* replacing a **field** that held it.

		~BT~drop(v, low, high)~BT~ removes ~BT~low~BT~ through ~BT~high~BT~ **inclusive** and its bounds
		are proven exactly as a slice's are. Crossed bounds take nothing.

		## A ~BT~Str~BT~'s own bounds

		A ~BT~Str~BT~ is indexed and sliced like a vector and proved like one, with a single
		difference: **a ~BT~Str~BT~ never has a length the compiler knows.**

		~CODE~hive
		s := "abc"
		echo s[1]                    // compile error, even though ~BT~s~BT~ is right there
		if s bounds 1 { echo s[1] }  // fine

		if indexOf(line, "=") is Result.Ok(at) {
			echo line[at:]           // no guard: the search proved it
		}
		~CODE~

		A slice's two bounds are proved separately, so a guard settling one says nothing
		about the other.

		*From ~BT~spec/10-bounds.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func generics(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Generics

		A name in a signature that is neither a builtin nor a declared type is a **type
		variable**, and it makes the callable generic in it.

		~CODE~hive
		func first(v: T[]): Result<T, Bool> {
			if len(v) > 0 {
				return Result.Ok(v[0])
			}
			return Result.Error(false)
		}
		~CODE~

		## Where a variable is pinned down

		By wherever it appears in the **parameters** — including *inside* a parameter's
		own type, which is what makes a higher-order generic work:

		~CODE~hive
		func filterMap(values: T[], transform: func(T): Result<K, E>): K[dyn] {
			mut K[dyn] out = []
			for each value in values {
				if transform(value) is Result.Ok(next) {
					append(out, next)
				}
			}
			return out
		}
		~CODE~

		The call says what ~BT~T~BT~ is by the vector it passes and what ~BT~K~BT~ and ~BT~E~BT~ are by the
		function it passes. **None of the three is written at the call site.** The body
		may write those variables down too — ~BT~mut K[dyn] out = []~BT~, ~BT~for each v: T in …~BT~,
		~BT~Box<T> b = …~BT~ — and each copy substitutes through its own body.

		## Generic types

		A type whose fields mention variables is generic in them, in **first-appearance
		order**, and is written out where it is used:

		~CODE~hive
		type Box {
			items: T[dyn]
			label: Str
		}

		type Either {
			Left  { left: A }
			Right { right: B }
		}

		Box<Str> people = Box(["ada", "grace"], "people")
		Either<Str, Int> answer = Either.Right(42)
		~CODE~

		The type arguments go on the **declaration of the value**, which is also what
		tells a variant constructor which instantiation it is building. Each
		instantiation gets its own clone, JSON codec and structural digest, so
		~BT~Either<Str, Int>~BT~ and ~BT~Either<Int, Str>~BT~ are different types all the way down
		to the wire.

		## Monomorphization

		Nothing about generics is dynamic. Every call site is resolved at compile time,
		with the type arguments read off the argument types, and **one concrete copy is
		emitted per distinct set of them** — ~BT~first_Str~BT~, ~BT~first_Int~BT~. No boxing, no
		dispatch, no reflection.

		Because each copy is an ordinary declaration, **every later check runs on it
		separately**: an instantiation at ~BT~Str[3]~BT~ is held to that length while one at
		~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ guards its indexes.

		## What is rejected

		* **A generic callable cannot be used as a value** — ~BT~f := first~BT~ is a compile
		  error, because which copy a call reaches is decided by the argument types and
		  a value carries none.
		* **A variable that appears only in the return type** has nothing to be inferred
		  from: ~BT~func makeOne(n: Int): T~BT~ is a compile error at the call.
		* **Runaway expansion is capped**, and overrunning the limit is a compile error
		  rather than a hung build.

		*From ~BT~spec/11-generics.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func modules(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Modules

		~BT~import~BT~, written outside any callable, brings another file's declarations into
		scope.

		~CODE~hive
		import ./lib/text
		import ./lib/inventory as stock

		proc main(): void {
			echo text.repeat("=", 28)
			echo stock.line(stock.Item("Beeswax", 450))
		}
		~CODE~

		## Four kinds of path, and the path says which

		| written | what it names |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~import ./lib/text~BT~ | a Hive file on this disk — ~BT~.hive~BT~ is **never** written |
		| ~BT~import ./lib/util.go~BT~ | a Go file on this disk — ~BT~.go~BT~ **always** is |
		| ~BT~import https://host/owner/repo/src/foo~BT~ | a file in a git repository |
		| ~BT~import hive.ui~BT~ | a standard library module, which names no file |

		A path may be **quoted** when it holds a space — ~BT~import "./lib/my file"~BT~ —
		and nothing inside needs escaping.

		## Names

		* The path is **relative to the importing file's own directory**, not to where
		  the compiler was run.
		* A module is reached through a name: the file's own by default
		  (~BT~./lib/text~BT~ → ~BT~text~BT~), or whatever ~BT~as~BT~ gives it. The name is ~BT~camelCase~BT~,
		  and it may not be ~BT~hive~BT~.
		* **Everything a module declares is visible.** There is no ~BT~pub~BT~/~BT~priv~BT~.

		## The graph

		A file is **loaded once** however many modules reach for it, and **import cycles
		are rejected** — direct or round any number of steps — with the loop printed.

		The whole program then becomes **one module**: the entrypoint keeps its own
		names and every imported module's declarations get a prefix of their own. Names
		carry **no baggage across a module boundary** — two files may each declare their
		own ~BT~Align~BT~ and both stay distinct types. An imported type is constructed,
		annotated and matched through the name its module is reached by
		(~BT~text.Align.Left()~BT~, a ~BT~text.Align~BT~ parameter, ~BT~style is text.Align.Left~BT~).

		Flattening has one cost: the passes after the loader report against the
		**entrypoint** rather than the file a declaration came from. Lexer, parser and
		import errors still name the right file.

		## Importing a Go file

		~CODE~hive
		import ./lib/measures.go

		proc main(): void {
			echo measures.grams(measures.Weight(899, "Hive tool"))
		}
		~CODE~

		* **Every value is deep-copied in both directions**, which is why an imported Go
		  function is a ~BT~func~BT~ rather than a ~BT~proc~BT~.
		* **Only what Go exports is reachable.** A callable is reached with its first
		  letter lowered — ~BT~Grams~BT~ is ~BT~measures.grams~BT~ — while a type keeps its
		  PascalCase.
		* The signatures are read by **the Go toolchain**, not guessed at.

		| Go | Hive |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~string~BT~ / ~BT~int~BT~ / ~BT~float64~BT~ / ~BT~bool~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ / ~BT~Int~BT~ / ~BT~Float~BT~ / ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
		| ~BT~[]T~BT~ | ~BT~T[dyn]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~[][]string~BT~ | ~BT~Table~BT~ |
		| ~BT~map[K]V~BT~ | ~BT~hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~, with a scalar key |
		| a struct the file exports | a Hive type of the same name and shape |
		| ~BT~(T, error)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<T, Str>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~error~BT~ alone | ~BT~Result<Bool, Str>~BT~, whose ~BT~Ok~BT~ carries ~BT~true~BT~ |
		| no result | a function answering with nothing |

		Hive's ~BT~Int~BT~ *is* Go's ~BT~int~BT~, so ~BT~int64~BT~ and the rest are **rejected** rather
		than silently converted. A pointer, channel, interface, function value, variadic
		parameter, fixed-size array, type from another package, or unexported field is
		refused with a message saying why; a struct crosses only when *every* field of
		it does. A Go map arrives with its keys **sorted**, having no order of its own.

		## Importing from a git repository

		~CODE~hive
		import https://github.com/owner/repo/src/text
		import https://github.com/owner/repo@a1b2c3/src/text        // pinned
		import https://github.com/owner/repo/go/util.go as helpers
		~CODE~

		* ~BT~https://host/owner/repo~BT~ is the repository and everything after it is the
		  path inside it. A revision goes where the repository ends: ~BT~repo@a1b2c3~BT~,
		  ~BT~repo@v1.2.0~BT~, ~BT~repo@main~BT~.
		* The module is **named after the file inside the repository**.
		* Everything is fetched **once**, into a cache shared by every program on the
		  machine.
		* An import naming no revision is **pinned on first use**, into a lock file
		  beside the entrypoint (~BT~main.hive~BT~ → ~BT~main.hive-lock~BT~). Keep it in version
		  control; delete a line to take the latest again.
		* Fetching needs ~BT~git~BT~ on the PATH.

		## Importing a standard library module

		~CODE~hive
		import hive.ui
		import hive.net as web
		~CODE~

		The path is the module and nothing else — ~BT~import hive.ui~BT~, never
		~BT~import hive.ui.View~BT~. **Nothing is imported into the program**: the alias is a
		*spelling*, ~BT~ui.row~BT~ and ~BT~hive.ui.row~BT~ are the same call, both are always
		available, and neither changes what is linked. ~BT~import hive~BT~ is not a thing to
		write.

		**A module you don't use is not in your build.** Each ~BT~hive.*~BT~ module is
		compiled and linked only when the program references it.

		*From ~BT~spec/12-modules.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func builtins(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Builtins

		Always in scope, no import needed. Several are overloaded by argument type.

		| function | signature | what it does |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| ~BT~len~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Str): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Map<K,T>): Int~BT~ | elements / **characters** / pairs |
		| ~BT~bytes~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): Int~BT~ / ~BT~(Str): Int~BT~ | byte footprint / **bytes** of the UTF-8 |
		| ~BT~append~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], T): void~BT~ | grows a mutable dynamic vector in place |
		| ~BT~prepend~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], T): void~BT~ | the same, at the **front** |
		| ~BT~drop~BT~ | ~BT~(mut T[dyn], Int, Int): T[dyn]~BT~ | removes ~BT~low~BT~–~BT~high~BT~ **inclusive**, hands them back |
		| ~BT~join~BT~ | ~BT~(Str[], Str): Str~BT~ | concatenates, ~BT~sep~BT~ between elements |
		| ~BT~split~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | splits on ~BT~sep~BT~ |
		| ~BT~replaceFirst~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str, Str): Str~BT~ | the first ~BT~from~BT~, rewritten |
		| ~BT~replaceAll~BT~ | ~BT~(Str, Str, Str): Str~BT~ | every ~BT~from~BT~, rewritten |
		| ~BT~indexOf~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], T): Result<Int, Bool>~BT~ / ~BT~(Str, Str): …~BT~ | position of the first match |
		| ~BT~row~BT~ | ~BT~(Table, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | the row whose first cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else ~BT~[]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~column~BT~ | ~BT~(Table, Str): Str[dyn]~BT~ | the column whose top cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else ~BT~[]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~map~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): K): K[dyn]~BT~ | every element, transformed |
		| ~BT~filter~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): Bool): T[dyn]~BT~ | the elements ~BT~keep~BT~ says yes to |
		| ~BT~filterMap~BT~ | ~BT~(T[], func(T): Result<K, E>): K[dyn]~BT~ | transform and select in one pass |
		| ~BT~sort~BT~ | ~BT~(T[]): T[dyn]~BT~ / ~BT~(T[], func(T, T): Bool): T[dyn]~BT~ | in order |

		~BT~len~BT~ and ~BT~bytes~BT~ differ only for strings: for ~BT~"café"~BT~, ~BT~len~BT~ is ~BT~4~BT~ and ~BT~bytes~BT~
		is ~BT~5~BT~. ~BT~replaceFirst~BT~ and ~BT~replaceAll~BT~ **answer with a new string**, take plain
		text rather than a pattern, and are not an error when ~BT~from~BT~ is absent.

		## A declaration of your own wins

		If your program declares a ~BT~func~BT~, ~BT~proc~BT~, ~BT~query~BT~ or type named ~BT~len~BT~, ~BT~map~BT~,
		~BT~join~BT~ — any of the names above — **that** is what your bare calls mean. The
		builtin is still there as ~BT~hive.<name>~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		func len(label: Str, extra: Int): Str { return "{label}{extra}" }

		proc main(): void {
			Str[dyn] v = ["a", "b"]
			echo len("x", 2)          // ours: "x2"
			echo hive.len(v)          // the builtin: 2
			echo hive.join(v, "-")    // the long name works either way
		}
		~CODE~

		A local binding and a parameter shadow one the same way, and shadowing is **per
		module**. Two things follow: only the builtin ~BT~append~BT~ requires a ~BT~mut~BT~ target,
		and only the builtin ~BT~indexOf~BT~ hands back an index the bounds pass accepts
		unguarded.

		## Walking a vector: ~BT~map~BT~, ~BT~filter~BT~, ~BT~filterMap~BT~

		Each takes a vector and a function over its elements and hands back a new one.

		* The function is a **~BT~func~BT~, never a ~BT~proc~BT~**. A walk says nothing about the
		  order its function runs in, so there is nowhere to hang a side effect. To walk
		  with a proc, write a ~BT~for each~BT~ loop.
		* Each is specific about what its function answers with: ~BT~filter~BT~ wants a
		  ~BT~Bool~BT~, ~BT~filterMap~BT~ a ~BT~Result~BT~, and ~BT~map~BT~ wants *something* — a ~BT~void~BT~
		  function collects nothing.
		* **A walk is sequential**, once per element, in order, on the calling thread.
		  To run a batch of calls together, write the
		  [await-all](concurrency.md).
		* ~BT~map~BT~ is **length-preserving** and the [bounds pass](bounds.md) knows it;
		  ~BT~filter~BT~ and ~BT~filterMap~BT~ are not.

		## Growing and shrinking

		All three write *through* a vector and all three ask for a **mutable dynamic**
		one (~BT~mut T[dyn]~BT~). ~BT~prepend~BT~ hands nothing back, so it may only stand as a
		statement. ~BT~drop~BT~ removes a range, both ends inclusive, and is the one builtin
		that makes a vector **shorter** — so it costs that vector whatever the bounds
		pass had proved.

		## ~BT~sort~BT~

		Hands back a new vector holding the same elements in order; the original is
		never touched. **One argument** orders by the element type's own order:

		| element | order |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~ | numeric, ascending |
		| ~BT~Str~BT~ | by UTF-8 byte order |
		| ~BT~Bool~BT~ | ~BT~false~BT~ before ~BT~true~BT~ |
		| ~BT~Atom~BT~ | by its compiled value |
		| a vector, a ~BT~Table~BT~ | lexicographic; on a shared prefix the shorter first |
		| ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~ | every ~BT~Error~BT~ before every ~BT~Ok~BT~ |
		| a struct | field by field, in declaration order |
		| a tagged union | by **variant** first, in declaration order, then its fields |
		| a function value, a service address | none — a compile error |

		**Two arguments** order by a ~BT~func~BT~ of your own answering whether its first
		element comes before its second — also the answer for an element type with no
		default order. The sort is **stable**, and both forms are length-preserving.

		### Sorting in place

		A ~BT~sort~BT~ written as a **statement** throws away the vector it answers with. When
		the vector it was given is ~BT~mut~BT~, that is taken at its word and the storage is
		reordered where it lies:

		~CODE~hive
		mut Str[dyn] names = ["pear", "apple"]

		sort(names)                  // in place — ~BT~names~BT~ is now sorted
		sorted := sort(names)        // NOT in place — a new vector, ~BT~names~BT~ untouched

		Int[dyn] v = [3, 1, 2]
		sort(v)                      // compile error: ~BT~v~BT~ is not ~BT~mut~BT~
		sort(split(line, ","))       // compile error: nothing to sort in place
		~CODE~

		Both conditions — discarded, and ~BT~mut~BT~ — do work, and a discarded ~BT~sort~BT~ that
		cannot sort in place is a compile error rather than a statement that quietly does
		nothing. The subject can be any ~BT~mut~BT~ storage: ~BT~sort(b.items)~BT~, ~BT~sort(rows[i])~BT~.

		*From ~BT~spec/13-builtins.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func tables(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Tables and ~BT~using~BT~

		~BT~Table~BT~ is an alias for ~BT~Str[dyn][dyn]~BT~ — a vector of rows of cells. It is what
		~BT~using~BT~ yields from a CSV, what a ~BT~run raw~BT~ query answers with, and what
		~BT~hive.map.toTable~BT~ builds. Two builtins read one by name:

		~CODE~hive
		row(table, key)      // the row whose first cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else []
		column(table, key)   // the column whose top cell equals ~BT~key~BT~, else []
		~CODE~

		## ~BT~using~BT~ reads a table

		Each form says **in the source** what it is reading, which is what lets the
		compiler pick the reader and leave the machinery for the others out of the
		build.

		~CODE~hive
		using "./data.csv"                            // a comma-separated CSV
		using "./data.tsv" as csv separating by "\t"  // another separator
		using "./book.xlsx" as xlsx                   // every sheet of a workbook
		using "./book.ods" as ods                     // every table of an ODS
		using db run allUsers()                       // a declared query, typed rows
		using db run raw someSqlText                  // SQL built at run time
		~CODE~

		| form | yields |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~using <path>~BT~ / ~BT~… as csv [separating by <sep>]~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, hive.TableError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~using <path> as xlsx~BT~ / ~BT~as ods~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], hive.TableError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~using <connection> run <query>~BT~ | whatever the query declared its rows to be |
		| ~BT~using <connection> run raw <text>~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ |

		A CSV is a single table, so it comes back as one ~BT~Table~BT~. **A spreadsheet holds
		many**, so xlsx and ods come back as a ~BT~Table[dyn]~BT~ — one per sheet, in document
		order, an empty sheet being an empty ~BT~Table~BT~ so the positions still line up.
		Both readers are dependency-free.

		A ~BT~hive.TableError~BT~ carries a ~BT~path~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~. It is one of the three
		types that live directly on ~BT~hive~BT~, alongside ~BT~Result~BT~ and ~BT~Table~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		if using "./colonies.csv" is Result.Ok(sheet) {
			for each line in sheet {
				echo join(line, " | ")
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		## What a spreadsheet cell arrives as

		As the file stores it, with one exception: **xlsx keeps a date as a day count**,
		so the cell's number format is consulted to catch exactly those and render them
		as ~BT~2026-07-25~BT~ (or with a time, or time-only). Numbers, booleans and cached
		formula results pass through untouched. An ODS stores real dates, so nothing has
		to be undone there. **Rows are padded to the widest row in their sheet.**

		## The operand is parsed at the postfix level

		So a call is consumed whole and none of ~BT~as~BT~, ~BT~csv~BT~, ~BT~run~BT~, ~BT~raw~BT~ or
		~BT~separating by~BT~ can be mistaken for an operator. None of them is a reserved
		word: each means something only in this clause.

		A bare ~BT~using <path>~BT~ is a comma-separated CSV.

		*From ~BT~spec/14-stdlib.md~BT~ §14.6 and ~BT~spec/05-expressions.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func queries(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Queries

		A ~BT~query~BT~ is a ~BT~func~BT~ whose body is **inline SQL** and whose **return type
		describes its rows**. It is run with ~BT~using <connection> run <query>~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		type User {
			id:   Int
			name: Str
		}

		query findUser(name: Str): User[dyn] {
			SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE name = {name}
		}

		query userNames(): Str[dyn] {      // one column needs no row type
			SELECT name FROM users
		}

		query deleteUser(id: Int): void {  // a statement reports what it touched
			DELETE FROM users WHERE id = {id}
		}
		~CODE~

		| declared | ~BT~using conn run q(...)~BT~ yields |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Row[dyn]~BT~ (a declared type) | ~BT~Result<Row[dyn], hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~, ~BT~Int[dyn]~BT~, … | that column, as a vector |
		| ~BT~void~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, hive.sql.SqlError>~BT~ — the rows it affected |

		## What the compiler enforces

		* **Columns match fields by name**, so reordering the ~BT~SELECT~BT~ cannot silently
		  remap them. A column whose name differs from its field needs an alias
		  (~BT~SELECT u.name AS author~BT~). A row type holds scalars only.
		* **~BT~SELECT *~BT~ against a declared row type is a compile error.** It says neither
		  how many columns come back nor what they are called. The rule is about the
		  *result*, so ~BT~count(*)~BT~, ~BT~a * 2~BT~, a star inside a subquery and a ~BT~void~BT~
		  statement's select list all still compile.
		* **Values are bound, never spliced.** An interpolated ~BT~{param}~BT~ becomes a
		  placeholder and the value travels beside the text, so nothing a caller
		  supplies can change what a statement means. Queries are written with ~BT~?~BT~ and
		  rewritten to ~BT~$1, $2, …~BT~ for PostgreSQL by the connection.

		## Optional filters: ~BT~WHERE { }~BT~

		A ~BT~WHERE~BT~ block ANDs the predicates whose conditions hold; a nested ~BT~or { }~BT~ or
		~BT~and { }~BT~ flips the connective:

		~CODE~hive
		query findColonies(apiary: Str, minFrames: Int, small: Bool, huge: Bool): Colony[dyn] {
			SELECT name, apiary, frames FROM colonies
			WHERE {
				if apiary != ""  { apiary = {apiary} }
				if minFrames > 0 { frames >= {minFrames} }
				or {
					if small { frames < 4 }
					if huge  { frames > 10 }
				}
			}
			ORDER BY name
		}
		~CODE~

		A group that contributes nothing disappears rather than leaving a dangling
		connective, and if no predicate is present at all there is **no ~BT~WHERE~BT~ clause**
		— so there is no ~BT~WHERE 1 = 1~BT~ to write. A group contributing more than one
		predicate is parenthesised. Every branch's text is fixed at compile time; only
		which branches are taken is decided at run time.

		## Two things a ~BT~WHERE~BT~ block cannot do

		A **column name or sort direction can never be a parameter** — ~BT~ORDER BY {col}~BT~
		would sort by a constant string — so make the choices a variant type and dispatch
		to one query per ordering. And **SQL you genuinely assemble yourself** goes
		through ~BT~run raw~BT~, which is untyped by construction and greppable by design.

		## Inside a query body

		It is SQL rather than Hive: SQL's own ~BT~--~BT~ and ~BT~/* */~BT~ comments are recognised,
		SQL keywords keep SQL's upper-case convention, and a name that collides with one
		is quoted the way SQL has always done it (~BT~SELECT "order" FROM …~BT~).

		Connections come from [~BT~hive.sql~BT~](stdlib/sql.md).

		*From ~BT~spec/04-declarations.md~BT~ §4.5.*
	`)
}

func testing(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Testing

		A test is a top-level declaration, alongside ~BT~proc~BT~, ~BT~func~BT~, ~BT~query~BT~ and ~BT~type~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		import ./cart

		test "an empty cart costs nothing" {
			cart.Item[0] empty = []
			assert cart.total(empty) == 0
		}
		~CODE~

		Run them with **~BT~hive test <entrypoint.hive>~BT~**. There is nothing to install and
		nothing to register: no framework, no third-party library, no ~BT~describe~BT~/~BT~it~BT~,
		and no assertion vocabulary beyond the ~BT~assert~BT~ the language already has.

		A test takes no parameters and returns nothing. Tests may live beside the code
		they are about or in a file of their own; a file holding only tests needs no
		~BT~main~BT~, and a run covers **every test in every file the entrypoint reaches**.

		## ~BT~assert~BT~ means the same thing and does something different

		In ordinary code a failed assertion has proved the **program** wrong, so it
		stops. Inside a test it has proved the **test** wrong, so the failure is recorded
		and the rest of the suite still runs. A test that **panics** fails on its own
		rather than taking the suite with it.

		A failed comparison shows both sides:

		~CODE~
		  PASS  an empty cart costs nothing
		  FAIL  lines add up
		        cart.test.hive:33: assert cart.total(two) == 17
		          left:  16
		          right: 17

		  2 tests: 1 passed, 1 failed
		~CODE~

		**An ~BT~assert~BT~'s operands are evaluated exactly once**, whether it holds or not,
		so an assertion over a call with an effect means what it looks like:

		~CODE~hive
		test "the cursor advances" {
			mut P p = P(tokens, 0, [])
			assert parseSum(p) == 42     // runs parseSum once, not twice
			assert len(p.errors) == 0
		}
		~CODE~

		## Coverage is not a separate command

		Every run reports it — there is no flag to remember:

		~CODE~
		  6 tests: 6 passed
		  coverage: 87.5% of statements (7/8)
		  never exercised: describe
		~CODE~

		The percentage counts statements of **your own** declarations; the clone
		helpers, ordering helpers, atom table and JSON marshalling the compiler emits
		are not something a test can be said to have missed. ~BT~never exercised~BT~ names
		the declarations no test reached at all. With more than one file, a per-file
		breakdown is printed too.

		A test run **exits non-zero when any test fails**, which is what makes it the
		thing a commit hook or a CI step runs.

		## Where a test runs

		A test runs with the working directory set to the **generated project**, not to
		the entrypoint's folder — a difference from ~BT~hive run~BT~, which chdirs to the
		entrypoint's folder so ~BT~using "./test.csv"~BT~ resolves the way its author wrote
		it.

		So **a test that opens a relative path is not looking where the source file
		is**. A test that needs files should write them itself —
		~BT~hive.file.makeDir~BT~ and ~BT~hive.file.write~BT~ into a directory of its own — which
		is also what makes it independent of how the suite was invoked.

		## What runs it

		~BT~go test~BT~. The tests are generated into a ~BT~main_test.go~BT~ in the **same Go
		package** as ~BT~main.go~BT~, which is what lets a test reach every proc, func and
		type the program declares without any of them being exported or otherwise made
		testable on purpose.

		*From ~BT~spec/16-testing.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

func cli(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# The ~BT~hive~BT~ command line

		~CODE~
		hive check     <entrypoint.hive>       Report any errors, build nothing
		hive emit      <entrypoint.hive>       Print the generated Go source
		hive build     <entrypoint.hive>       Compile to a native executable
		hive run       <entrypoint.hive> [..]  Compile and run
		hive test      <entrypoint.hive>       Run the program's tests, with coverage
		hive container <entrypoint.hive>       Write a Dockerfile that builds and runs it
		hive agents                            Write .hivedocs/ for coding agents
		hive version                           Print which release this compiler is

		  --target <goos>/<goarch>             Build for another platform, on ~BT~build~BT~ alone
		~CODE~

		The entrypoint is the file holding ~BT~proc main(): void~BT~, and a program is that
		file plus everything it imports.

		## Which to reach for

		* **~BT~hive check~BT~** runs every compiler pass and stops before the Go toolchain, so
		  it answers as fast as the front end does and writes nothing next to the file.
		  It is the one to run on every save, and the one to run after an edit.
		* **~BT~hive test~BT~** runs the suite with coverage and **exits non-zero when any
		  test fails**.
		* **~BT~hive run~BT~** compiles and runs, forwarding everything after the entrypoint
		  to the program and chdir'ing to the entrypoint's folder. The program's exit
		  status becomes the command's.
		* **~BT~hive build~BT~** leaves the executable beside the entrypoint and the generated
		  Go module in ~BT~<entrypoint>-build/~BT~.
		* **~BT~hive emit~BT~** prints the Go to stdout, so ~BT~hive emit x.hive > main.go~BT~
		  works — progress goes to stderr for exactly that reason.

		~BT~--target~BT~ belongs to ~BT~build~BT~ and nothing else; ~BT~go tool dist list~BT~ names every
		platform it takes. A flag a command does not know is **refused rather than
		quietly dropped**.

		## Diagnostics

		Every error opens with where it happened, and nothing is printed in front of it:

		~CODE~
		main.hive:12: cannot prove this index is in range: the index is a computed
		    expression. Bind it to a variable and guard it (~BT~if j >= 0 && j < len(v)~BT~).
		~CODE~

		~BT~file:line: message~BT~, with a message that runs on continuing on **indented**
		lines so one error stays one entry in a quickfix list. For Vim or Neovim:

		~CODE~vim
		setlocal makeprg=hive\ check\ %:S
		setlocal errorformat=%-G\ %.%#,%f:%l:\ %m,%-G%.%#
		~CODE~

		**Two limits worth knowing.** Errors from the passes after parsing land on a
		**declaration** rather than a statement, and in a program with imports those
		same errors are reported against the **entrypoint** even when the declaration at
		fault came from an imported module — flattening is what discards which file it
		came from. Lexer, parser and import errors do name the right file and line.

		## ~BT~hive agents~BT~

		Writes this documentation. It takes no entrypoint and no flags, rewrites its own
		files under ~BT~.hivedocs/~BT~ from the compiler that is running, and leaves anything
		else in that directory alone. It also creates an ~BT~AGENTS.md~BT~ if there is none,
		adds ~BT~.hivedocs/~BT~ to a ~BT~.gitignore~BT~ that has one, and puts ~BT~@AGENTS.md~BT~ at the
		top of a ~BT~CLAUDE.md~BT~ that does not open with it. **Run it again after upgrading
		the compiler.**

		*From ~BT~spec/17-diagnostics.md~BT~ and the compiler's own ~BT~src/hivec.hive~BT~.*
	`)
}

func pitfalls(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# Pitfalls

		The refusals a program written in another language's habits runs into. Every one
		below is a **compile error**, and each is followed by what to write instead.

		## Conditions and operators

		**There is no truthiness.** A condition is a ~BT~Bool~BT~; an ~BT~Atom~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Str~BT~ or
		vector there is an error. → ~BT~if flag == #Ready~BT~, ~BT~if len(v) > 0~BT~, ~BT~if s != ""~BT~.

		**~BT~!~BT~ inverts a ~BT~Bool~BT~ and nothing else** — there being no truthiness, there is
		nothing about an ~BT~Int~BT~ or an ~BT~Atom~BT~ to be the opposite of.

		**~BT~!~BT~ binds tight**, at unary ~BT~-~BT~'s level, so anything looser needs parentheses:
		~BT~!(a == b)~BT~, ~BT~!(v bounds i)~BT~. ~BT~!a && b~BT~ is ~BT~(!a) && b~BT~, and
		~BT~!x is Shape.Point~BT~ is ~BT~(!x) is …~BT~ — a type error, not what it looks like.

		**A match cannot be negated.** ~BT~!(x is Shape.Circle(r))~BT~ is an error: ~BT~is~BT~
		narrows and binds, and neither survives a ~BT~!~BT~, so ~BT~r~BT~ could never be set. → put
		the other case in the ~BT~else~BT~.

		**No number is widened for you.** ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ is an error
		because those calls take ~BT~Float~BT~. → ~BT~hive.conv.itf(0)~BT~.

		**Comparing two different types** is an error, not a silent ~BT~false~BT~.

		## Vectors

		**~BT~[dyn]~BT~ is declared, never inferred.** ~BT~mut v := ["a"]~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[1]~BT~ and
		~BT~append~BT~ing to it is an error. → ~BT~mut Str[dyn] v = ["a"]~BT~.

		**~BT~append~BT~, ~BT~prepend~BT~ and ~BT~drop~BT~ need a ~BT~mut~BT~ target.** An immutable binding
		cannot be written through at all.

		**Every index must be proved.** ~BT~v[len(v) - 1]~BT~ is a computed index and is
		refused. → bind it and guard it: ~BT~last := len(v) - 1~BT~, then
		~BT~if v bounds last { v[last] }~BT~.

		**A slice's high bound is inclusive.** ~BT~v[0:2]~BT~ is **three** elements, not two.

		**~BT~filter~BT~ and ~BT~filterMap~BT~ lose the length** for good — no index into their
		result is ever provable without a guard.

		**~BT~Str[]~BT~ is a parameter spelling only.** A return and anything naming storage
		must say ~BT~[dyn]~BT~ or ~BT~[n]~BT~.

		## Strings

		**A ~BT~Str~BT~ never has a length the compiler knows**, so even ~BT~s := "abc"~BT~ needs
		~BT~if s bounds 1~BT~ before ~BT~s[1]~BT~. An index from ~BT~indexOf~BT~ needs no guard.

		**~BT~s[0] = "x"~BT~ is an error** — a ~BT~Str~BT~ is a value, not storage. Build a new one.

		**Indexing a ~BT~Str~BT~ counts characters, not bytes**, and yields a one-character
		~BT~Str~BT~. ~BT~len~BT~ counts characters; ~BT~bytes~BT~ counts bytes.

		## Names and declarations

		**A miscased keyword is neither a keyword nor a name.** A union variant called
		~BT~Return~BT~, ~BT~Break~BT~ or ~BT~Echo~BT~, or a field called ~BT~type~BT~, is an error. →
		~BT~ReturnStmt~BT~.

		**A ~BT~mut~BT~ variable may not be ~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~**, a counting loop's counter
		included. The rule runs that way only: an immutable variable **may** be
		~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ and is never obliged to be — ~BT~camelCase~BT~ is the default for every
		variable, and ~BT~retries := 3~BT~ needs no shouting to hold still.

		**~BT~UPPER_CASE~BT~ is for variables and nothing else.** A parameter, a field, a
		callable, an import's name and a name bound by a pattern or a ~BT~for each~BT~ are
		~BT~camelCase~BT~, however fixed the value behind them is.

		**A ~BT~func~BT~ cannot call a ~BT~proc~BT~**, and cannot declare a ~BT~mut~BT~ parameter. If a
		body needs either, it is a ~BT~proc~BT~.

		**Every non-~BT~void~BT~ path must return.** An ~BT~if~BT~/~BT~else if~BT~ chain over a whole
		union counts — and **adding an ~BT~else~BT~ to an exhaustive one is itself an error**.

		**~BT~mut~BT~ goes nowhere but a declaration and a ~BT~proc~BT~ parameter**: not on a field,
		not on a return, not in a function type.

		## Matching

		**There is no ~BT~match~BT~ or ~BT~case~BT~.** Every match is ~BT~if x is …~BT~ / ~BT~else if~BT~.

		**Name every field of a variant or none** — a partly written argument list is
		refused. Use ~BT~_~BT~ for the ones you do not want.

		**Vector and string patterns never make a chain exhaustive**, so a function
		matching on those still needs a terminating ~BT~else~BT~ or a final ~BT~return~BT~.

		**Two open string-pattern holes may not sit side by side** — ~BT~"{a}{b}"~BT~ is
		ambiguous. Put a literal between them, or give one a regex.

		**A binding from ~BT~is~BT~ is out of scope in the ~BT~else~BT~.**

		## Concurrency

		**~BT~async~BT~ is not an expression.** It appears as a statement of its own and on
		the value side of a declaration, and nowhere else.

		**Every entry of an ~BT~await~BT~ must be a call**, they must all answer with the same
		type, and ~BT~await []~BT~ is an error.

		**A ~BT~void~BT~ call takes neither an ~BT~async~BT~ binding nor ~BT~with timeout~BT~** — there
		would be nothing for the name or the ~BT~Result~BT~ to hold.

		**An ~BT~async~BT~ call gets a copy of a ~BT~mut~BT~ argument**, not the caller's storage.

		## Collections and modules

		**~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~ must land somewhere that says what it holds.**
		~BT~m := hive.map.new()~BT~ is an error. → ~BT~mut hive.map.Map<Str, Int> m = hive.map.new()~BT~.

		**A map has no subscript and no ~BT~for each~BT~.** → ~BT~hive.map.get~BT~, and loop over
		~BT~hive.map.keys(m)~BT~.

		**A Hive import never writes ~BT~.hive~BT~; a Go import always writes ~BT~.go~BT~.**

		**An import may not be named ~BT~hive~BT~**, and ~BT~import hive~BT~ is not a thing to
		write — the library is reached a module at a time.

		## Generics and values

		**A generic callable cannot be used as a value**, and neither can one with a
		mutex parameter or a statically-sized parameter it is handed on from.

		**A type variable that appears only in the return type** cannot be inferred.

		## Queries

		**~BT~SELECT *~BT~ against a declared row type is an error**, and columns match
		fields **by name** — alias anything spelled differently.

		**A column name or a sort direction can never be a ~BT~{param}~BT~.**

		## Sorting

		**~BT~b := sort(a)~BT~ does not sort ~BT~a~BT~** — it answers with a new vector. Only a
		**discarded** ~BT~sort~BT~ on a ~BT~mut~BT~ vector sorts in place, and a discarded one that
		cannot is an error rather than dead code.

		*Every rule here is stated in full on the page for its feature; start at
		[README](README.md).*
	`)
}
